# Design vs Dev Comparison Documents

When creating a comparison audit between a Pencil design and its dev implementation, follow this structure and methodology.

## Directory Structure

```
reviews/{screen-name}-comparison/
  build-standalone.mjs     # Node.js script that generates the HTML
  PFM-{Name}-Design-vs-Dev.html  # Output (self-contained, base64 images)
  index.html               # Lightweight redirect/alias
  assets/
    design/                # Exported PNGs from Pencil (frame IDs as filenames)
    dev/                   # Screenshots captured from dev site
    dev/modals/            # Modal/overlay dev screenshots
```

## Build Script Pattern

The `build-standalone.mjs` script:
1. Flattens design PNGs onto white background (PIL via Python)
2. Embeds all images as base64 data URIs (fully self-contained HTML)
3. Defines a Hemlock token map (`T` object) with exact values from the design system
4. Defines comparison row arrays per section
5. Uses helper functions (`compareSection`, `refSection`) to generate HTML
6. Outputs a single `.html` file

## Comparison Row Format

Each row is a 5-element array:

```javascript
['Item name', 'Design spec (Hemlock token)', 'Current dev state', 'Required change in dev', 'Gap|Match|Check']
```

### Rules for Row Content

- **Design column**: Use EXACT token name + value. Example: `spacing-md (24px)`. Never use ranges ("20–24px") or tildes ("~24px").
- **Dev column**: State the measured value from the dev site. Use CDP inspection for precision.
- **Required change column**: Specific instruction an engineer can act on. No vague language.
- **Status**: `Gap` = design and dev differ, `Match` = identical, `Verify` = needs verification against live dev.

## Sourcing Design Assets

1. Use Pencil MCP tools (`get_editor_state`, `snapshot_layout`, `batch_get`, `export_nodes`) to inspect and export frames
2. Export frames by their Pencil node ID (e.g., `FglEA.png`)
3. Flatten transparent PNGs onto white with Python PIL
4. Verify padding/spacing values directly from Pencil data (`padding`, `gap` properties) — don't guess

## Sourcing Dev Assets

1. Use the Cursor browser resized to the correct viewport (390px for mobile, full-width for desktop)
2. Navigate to the screen and capture with `browser_take_screenshot`
3. For transient states (hover, pressed), inject CSS via `browser_cdp` Runtime.evaluate
4. For measurements, use `browser_cdp` to query computed styles and element dimensions
5. For modals: navigate to trigger the modal, then screenshot

## Precision Requirements

- All spacing/padding values must reference exact Hemlock tokens (spacing-xs=12, spacing-sm=16, spacing-md=24, spacing-lg=32, spacing-xl=40)
- All font sizes must state the exact px value and weight
- All colors must include the token name AND hex value
- Button specs must include height, padding, border-radius, font details
- No approximations in the Design column — verify against Pencil source

## HTML Structure

- **Overview/cover section** at top: purpose, how-to-read, key rules, sources, stats (gap count, match count, total)
- **Fixed left sidebar navigation** (see detailed spec below)
- **Sections grouped by feature** (e.g., "Accounts list", "Detail modal") with Desktop/Mobile sub-sections
- Each section has: screenshot comparison (design vs dev side-by-side) + specification table
- Mobile sections must explicitly state: text wraps (never truncates), touch targets ≥44px, no horizontal scroll

## Side Navigation (Required)

Every comparison document MUST include a fixed left sidebar navigation. This is critical for usability on large documents.

### Structure

```html
<nav class="side-nav">
  <a href="#top" class="nav-link nav-top">↑ Overview</a>
  <span class="side-nav-title t-{category}">Category Name</span>
  <span class="nav-sub">Desktop</span>
  <a href="#s1" class="nav-link">1. Section name</a>
  ...
  <span class="nav-sub">Mobile</span>
  <a href="#s14" class="nav-link">14. Mobile section name</a>
  ...
</nav>
```

### Requirements

1. **Always present in the HTML** — never omit the `<nav>` element
2. **"Overview" link** at the top (links to `#top`) — allows jumping back to the cover section from anywhere
3. **Category headers** (`side-nav-title`) — color-coded by feature area (list, states, detail, modals, mobile, reference)
4. **Desktop/Mobile sub-labels** (`nav-sub`) — within each category, clearly separate desktop and mobile sections
5. **Links to every section** — each `<a>` links to the corresponding `<section id="s{num}">`
6. **Responsive behavior** — visible at ≥1200px viewport width; hidden below 1200px. Content area gets `margin-left: 200px` when sidebar is visible.
7. **Toggle button (required)** — when sidebar is hidden (below 1200px), a fixed hamburger button (top-left corner) must be visible so users can toggle the sidebar open. The page should NEVER look like navigation doesn't exist. When the sidebar is open, the toggle button must shift to the right edge of the sidebar (e.g., `left: 192px`) so it does not overlap the navigation text.
8. **Fixed position** — `position: fixed; top: 0; left: 0; height: 100vh; overflow-y: auto`

### Color-coded category classes

- `.t-list` — green (#2d6a4f) for main list/page sections
- `.t-states` — gold (#b8860b) for row interaction states
- `.t-data` — indigo (#6366f1) for data/content sections
- `.t-detail` — cyan (#0891b2) for detail views/popups
- `.t-modals` — red (#9b2226) for modal dialogs
- `.t-mobile` — purple (#7c3aed) for mobile-specific sections
- `.t-ref` — blue (#2563eb) for reference/checklist sections

## Image Display Rules

- **Never crop images.** All screenshots and design exports must use `object-fit: contain` so they scale proportionally without being cut off at any viewport width.
- **No `object-fit: cover`** on comparison images. This causes cropping when the viewport is resized.
- **No `max-height` constraints** that would clip image content. If an image is tall, let it be tall.
- **Only crop when explicitly requested** by the user (e.g., "crop to just the header area"). Default is always: show the full image.
- Panel images use: `display: block; width: 100%; height: auto; object-fit: contain;`

## Mobile-Specific Documentation

- Document overlay-to-modal padding (typically spacing-md / 24px from viewport edges)
- Document scroll behavior (only ONE scroll region allowed)
- Document text wrapping (no truncation with ellipsis on mobile)
- Document touch target compliance (min 44px for interactive elements)
- Document header borders (design typically has none; dev may add them)
